      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I'd like to propose some hardening on this issue.\nPrivate keys would be used to sign messages/strings to be sent to other users. A function would be named like SignMessage(string input). It would create a concatenated string of at least world id, world version, world asset hash, unique instance id, user id, current datetime, a nonce and the input, then hash (actually, HMAC input with the extra data as key) and sign this with the private key and return an opaque string containing the signature, user id, current datetime and nonce that can be sent over the network.\nPublic keys would be used to verify message/strings with a function like VerifyMessage(string input, string signature), it would extract signature, user id, current datetime and nonce from the signature, do some sanity checks, like the user id even existing in the session, the current datetime being close to actual current datetime and the nonce not having been used by the user id yet. If those checks are good, recreate the concatenated string from the SignMessage call, creating the hash and compare it to the decrypted hash from the signature. If they are the same, return the user id, else return null or error.\nAlso an important information that needs to be added to the documentation is, to never use SignMessage on strings sent by others over the network. Always generate the input at the client.\nTo improve authenticity inside VRChat network stack, I'd suggest to generate a DH-key pair during login and sending the public key part to login servers. When a client connects to an instance, it'd receive the instance's public key. Then they use DH key agreement to create a shared secret, which they can modify (like PBKDF) to use as key to sign network packets. This key pair cannot be used securely with the SignMessage method above though.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
